articles=[('planning-a-mobile-mvp','Planning a mobile MVP','Product planning',['Start with one useful outcome','Write down the problem, who faces it and the action your app should help them complete. This becomes the test for every proposed feature.','Separate essentials from later ideas','Identify the smallest end-to-end journey that delivers value. Authentication, payments and notifications should be included because that journey needs them, rather than by default.','Plan the work around review points','Review the user flow before visual design, the prototype before development and the main journeys before release. A short feedback loop makes changes easier to manage.']),('choosing-flutter','When Flutter fits your product','Mobile engineering',['Look at the platforms you need','Flutter can support a shared application codebase across Android and iOS. The product still needs attention to platform-specific permissions, integrations and store requirements.','Check the difficult integrations early','Prototype camera, Bluetooth, payments, background work or device connections before committing to a broad feature plan. Those integrations often shape implementation decisions.','Think beyond the first release','A clear architecture and deliberate state-management approach help the app remain understandable as the product grows. Plan maintenance alongside development.']),('adding-ai-to-your-app','Adding AI with a clear purpose','AI products',['Choose a concrete user task','Start with a workflow such as understanding an image, drafting text or answering a question from context. Define what a successful output looks like.','Design for uncertainty','AI results can be incomplete or wrong. Give users a way to review, correct or retry the output, and avoid presenting uncertain results as guarantees.','Plan cost and data handling','Estimate request volumes, define what data is sent to providers and protect API credentials on a backend. Consider latency, usage limits and failure states before launch.'])]

